is_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If a power series has radius of convergence $R = 0$ and centre $a = -2$, what does the say?","options":["It converges solely at $x = -2$","It converges uniformly on every closed interval","It converges pointwise on all of $\\mathbb{R}$","It has derivative series converging on $(a-R,a+R)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e61fe6745fda057410df01f","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For a power series centred at $a = 3$ with $R = \\frac{1}{2}$, choose $r = \\frac{1}{4}$. On which interval is uniform convergence guaranteed?","options":["$[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}]$","$(\\frac{11}{4},\\frac{13}{4})$","$[\\frac{11}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]$","$[3,\\frac{13}{4}]$ only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"50c5d8726adb0903d6515d92","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $R = 2$ and $a = 1$. Which closed interval is covered by the power-series convergence theorem's uniform convergence statement with $r = \\frac{3}{2}$?","options":["$[-1,3]$","$( -\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})$","$[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}]$","$[1,2]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b076ba4fafd8b66bbbfe383d","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which boundary warning is consistent with the power-series convergence theorem?","options":["The theorem does not by itself decide convergence at $x = a-R$ or $x = a+R$","The theorem says the series diverges at both $x = a-R$ and $x = a+R$","The theorem gives convergence at $x=a\\pm R$ but not uniform convergence","The theorem gives uniform convergence on the whole closed interval $[a-R,a+R]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"304f13ef180f4444f9f9c2b6","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If a power series has $R=+\\infty$, what can be concluded about uniform convergence on all of $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["It is automatic from $R = +\\infty$","It needs further analysis","It holds only on bounded subintervals","It occurs only at $x = -2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b1a93acac9dbbedb599be41f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For the series centred at $a = 3$ with $R = \\frac{1}{2}$, which point is inside the open interval of pointwise convergence?","options":["$x = \\frac{7}{2}$","$x = \\frac{17}{5}$","$x = \\frac{5}{2}$","$x = \\frac{18}{5}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7517bb3ac4bb7bdd90987318","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $R = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $a = 3$. In the power-series convergence theorem's uniform convergence statement, which value of $r$ is not allowed?","options":["$r = \\frac{1}{4}$","$r = \\frac{1}{2}$","$r = \\frac{1}{10}$","$r = \\frac{2}{5}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cc2b22ace8a91802ef4b9b11","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must $r$ satisfy $0 \\lt r \\lt R$ in the uniform convergence statement?","options":["So $[a-r,a+r]$ stays strictly inside the radius of convergence.","So that $r$ is exactly the endpoint of the interval of convergence.","So the series converges only at $a-r$ and $a+r$.","So the radius of convergence becomes $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"490e393f81a6e3d94186b3ac","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the power-series convergence theorem say about differentiability of the limit function inside the convergence interval?","options":["The limit function is nowhere differentiable.","The limit function is differentiable on $(a-R,a+R)$.","The limit function is differentiable only at the endpoint $a-R$.","The limit function is differentiable only if $R=0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"01610b38eff47445f7191197","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_convergence_of_power_series_82959e2b_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A power series is centred at $a=0$ with radius $R=3$. What is the open interval of pointwise convergence